ELKS fortune + BOFH, version 1.0

Native 8086/8088 executable, compiled for ELKS small-memory model.
The commands are fortune, fortune computers, fortune bofh, and bofh.
Use -n NUMBER to select a particular entry, or --help for usage.
This is a compact new reader, not a full port of fortune-mod's command line.

INSTALL (as root, after extracting this archive)
  mkdir -p /usr/lib/fortune
  cp bin/fortune /bin/fortune
  cp bin/bofh /bin/bofh
  chmod 755 /bin/fortune /bin/bofh
  cp lib/fortune/* /usr/lib/fortune/

The archive has no automatic installer and does not change startup files.
For a fortune on login, add /bin/fortune to your own shell profile if wanted.
Keep the accompanying licenses with any redistribution.

DATA
431 general fortunes, 1066 computer fortunes, 453 BOFH excuses.
The general/computer collections are from fortune-mod; individual entries
retain their attributions. The BOFH collection is distributed by Debian
as public domain. These classic collections may contain dated language.
This product includes software developed by the University of
California, Berkeley and its contributors.

Indices avoid scanning whole collections on an 8088. Keep each data file
and its .idx file together. If you edit a data file, remove its .idx or
regenerate it on a modern host using src/mkindex.py FILE. A custom plain
text file can be selected by path, with a line containing % separating
entries. An unindexed file is scanned twice, using constant small memory.
FORTUNE_DIR overrides the default /usr/lib/fortune location.

BUILD
In src, run make ELKS_ROOT=/path/to/elks TOOLCHAIN=/path/to/toolchain.
An ELKS ia16-elf-gcc toolchain and its built libc are required.
Run make host for a native-host reader, not the ELKS executable.
The prebuilt executables require no Python or compiler on the target.
